/** @page krb5usecases Use Cases in the %Qore and Qorus Ecosystem

    @tableofcontents

    The \c krb5 module is not only for
    <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/active-directory-domain-services-overview">Active
    Directory</a>. Its classes are general
    <a href="https://web.mit.edu/kerberos/">Kerberos 5</a> and
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2743">GSSAPI</a>
    primitives, and they apply everywhere enterprise services speak
    Kerberos — which is most of the Unix and Windows server ecosystem.

    This page describes the categories of integration the module enables.
    For runnable code, see @ref krb5examples.

    @section krb5uc_sso Client SSO to Enterprise Services

    The same initiator loop built around
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext::step() "GssClientContext::step()"
    works for any SASL/GSSAPI or SPNEGO peer. The module carries the tokens;
    the calling code carries them across whichever transport the peer uses.

    Typical targets:

    - **<a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4511">LDAP</a>
      with <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4752">SASL
      GSSAPI</a>** — Active Directory, Red Hat Identity Management,
      <a href="https://www.openldap.org/">OpenLDAP</a>, and 389 Directory
      Server. The channel-binding options enable
      <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5929">RFC 5929</a>
      \c tls-server-end-point binding for LDAP over TLS, which defeats
      TLS-proxy relay attacks.
    - **<a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4559">HTTP SPNEGO
      / Negotiate</a>** — corporate REST APIs behind
      <a href="https://github.com/gssapi/mod_auth_gssapi">mod_auth_gssapi</a>,
      nginx-krb5, and similar gateways. Hex-encoded GSSAPI tokens are
      transformed to base64 and placed in \c "Authorization: Negotiate …"
      headers by the caller.
    - **Databases** —
      <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/gssapi-auth.html">PostgreSQL</a>,
      Oracle,
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/native-client/features/using-integrated-authentication">Microsoft
      SQL Server</a>,
      <a href="https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/db2">IBM Db2</a>, and Sybase
      all support GSSAPI authentication. A
      @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential imported from a service keytab lets
      datasources connect with no stored password.
    - **<a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#security_sasl_kerberos">Kafka</a>**
      with \c SASL_GSSAPI.
    - **<a href="https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/ibm-mq">IBM MQ</a> / JMS**,
      **ActiveMQ**, and
      **<a href="https://www.rabbitmq.com/auth-mechanism-gssapi.html">RabbitMQ</a>**
      with the krb5 plugin.
    - **<a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4752">IMAP /
      POP3 / SMTP</a>** with SASL GSSAPI for mail-polling interfaces.
    - **<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-smb2/">SMB
      / CIFS</a>** corporate file shares (through a native file-share
      library layered on top of the module's GSSAPI primitives).

    From the module's perspective these integrations all look the same:
    acquire a credential (from cache or keytab), construct a
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext bound to the target service principal,
    and drive the step loop exchanging hex-encoded tokens with the peer.
    The default target-name type is a Kerberos principal such as
    \c "HTTP/qorus.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM"; set
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContextOptions::name_type to
    \c "hostbased-service" when a peer API expects the portable GSSAPI
    host-based form such as \c "HTTP@qorus.example.com".

    @section krb5uc_identity Passwordless Service Identity

    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context::acquireCredentialsWithKeytab() combined with
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential::fromKeytab() gives a %Qore or Qorus
    service a durable passwordless identity. Every outbound Kerberized
    connector — LDAP, database, HTTP, Kafka, MQ — can share the same
    credential instead of each subsystem carrying its own stored password.

    This is a significant operational improvement: credential rotation is
    driven by keytab rollover rather than by reconfiguring every integration
    interface.

    @section krb5uc_channel_binding Channel-Bound Hardening

    The channel-binding options in
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContextOptions — in particular
    \c channel_binding_application_data_hex carrying the
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5929">RFC 5929</a>
    \c tls-server-end-point value — let applications cryptographically bind
    their authentication to the TLS endpoint they are actually talking to.
    This defeats credential relay attacks through hostile TLS terminators
    and is a direct compliance improvement for deployments behind strict
    enterprise security policies.

    @section krb5uc_message_protection Message-Level Integrity and Confidentiality

    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext::wrap() and
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext::unwrap() expose negotiated SASL
    security layers at the application level. This enables per-message
    integrity or confidentiality independent of TLS — useful where messages
    traverse intermediaries that must see headers but must not see payload,
    where audit trails must be cryptographically sealed, or where replay
    protection is required at the application boundary.

    @section krb5uc_connections Qorus Connection Abstraction

    The module is a natural fit for a Qorus \c Connection subclass that
    lazily acquires a ticket from a configured keytab and hands the
    resulting @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential to any dependent connection
    (LDAP, database, HTTP, Kafka, and so on). This collapses "Kerberos for
    X, Kerberos for Y, Kerberos for Z" into a single identity-configuration
    primitive managed in one place.

    @section krb5uc_audit Audit and Identity Normalization

    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Principal parsing — including the enterprise
    (<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/ad/naming-properties">UPN</a>)
    form used by Active Directory logins — gives Qorus a canonical,
    transport-independent way to record \e who performed an action. The
    same principal representation works whether the operation arrived over
    HTTP SPNEGO, LDAP, a database connection, or a message bus, so audit
    records stay consistent across integrations.

    @section krb5uc_tests Deterministic Kerberos Tests

    The in-memory credential-cache and keytab backends, together with the
    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Credentials constructor that synthesises credentials
    from explicit hex values, make it possible to write integration tests
    for any of the above targets without depending on a live KDC. This is a
    quiet but significant ergonomics advantage over equivalent Python and
    Java stacks, where Kerberos tests typically require a containerized KDC.

    @section krb5uc_delegation Credential Delegation

    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssAcceptorContext::getDelegatedCredential()
    "GssAcceptorContext::getDelegatedCredential()" lets a service endpoint
    extract the delegated @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential after a GSSAPI
    exchange where the initiator set \c GSS_DELEG_FLAG. The extracted
    credential can then be used to initiate Kerberized connections to
    further backends on behalf of the original caller.

    This is a building block for multi-hop authentication scenarios: a Qorus
    workflow step can accept a user's Kerberos identity, extract the
    delegated credential, and use it to connect to an LDAP directory or
    database as that user rather than as the service account. Because
    delegation passes real Kerberos credentials, audit trails on the
    downstream service record the original user, not the intermediary.

    @note Delegation should only be enabled when the trust relationship
    between initiator and acceptor is well understood. See
    @ref krb5sec_delegation for the security implications.

    @section krb5uc_key_rotation Keytab Key Rotation

    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Keytab::removeEntry() "Krb5Keytab::removeEntry()"
    enables controlled key rotation workflows. The recommended pattern is:

    -# Add the new key version to the keytab with
       @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Keytab::addEntry() "addEntry()".
    -# Validate that the new key works for authentication.
    -# Remove the old key version with \c removeEntry().

    This overlapping-key window ensures that in-flight requests
    authenticated with the old key still succeed during the transition. See
    @ref krb5examples_keytab_rotation for a runnable example.

    @section krb5uc_http_spnego HTTP SPNEGO Authentication

    The \c Krb5Util helper module provides
    \c Krb5Util::negotiateSpnegoToken() and
    \c Krb5Util::parseSpnegoResponse() for constructing and parsing
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4178">RFC 4178</a>
    SPNEGO envelopes. Combined with the
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext token loop and the
    @ref Qore::Krb5::GssAcceptorContext acceptor, %Qore applications can
    implement both sides of
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4559">HTTP Negotiate</a>
    authentication natively:

    - **Client-side**: the initiator calls
      @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext::step() "step()", wraps the
      resulting GSSAPI token in SPNEGO with \c negotiateSpnegoToken(), and
      sends it as an \c "Authorization: Negotiate" header.
    - **Server-side**: the acceptor unwraps the SPNEGO envelope, feeds the
      inner GSSAPI token to
      @ref Qore::Krb5::GssAcceptorContext::step() "step()", wraps the
      response in SPNEGO, and sends it back as a
      \c "WWW-Authenticate: Negotiate" header.

    See @ref krb5examples_spnego_negotiate for a runnable example.

    @section krb5uc_extensions Natural Extensions

    These workflows fit naturally on top of the existing primitives:

    - **Constrained delegation
      (<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-sfu/3bff5864-8135-400e-bdd9-33b552051d94">S4U2Self
      / S4U2Proxy</a>)** — Qorus can use GSS-level S4U2Self helpers to act
      as an identity-forwarding service that authenticates to downstream
      Kerberized resources on behalf of the end user. The low-level
      Kerberos S4U2Proxy binding is available when the caller already has a
      Kerberos evidence credential.
    - **Credential maintenance** — long-running services can renew TGTs and
      prefetch service tickets through
      \c Krb5Util::maintainCredentialCache().
    - **Cross-realm trust** — already reachable today through KDC
      canonicalization and explicit realm handling on principals; useful
      for multi-tenant and post-merger deployments.
